export const LESSONS={
returns:{eyebrow:'STEP 02 · 25 MINUTES',title:'Count the whole<br>return.',lead:'A price change is only part of the story when an investment pays you cash.',objective:'Calculate a one-period total return and identify what it includes.',body:`<section class="panel"><span class="badge">ONE PERIOD · NO EXTERNAL DEPOSITS OR WITHDRAWALS</span><h2 style="margin-top:16px">Separate a pound gain from a return.</h2><p>Suppose you buy one hypothetical share at £50. At the end of the year it is worth £52, and you have received a £1 cash distribution. Your price gain is £2. Your total gain is £3. Divide by the £50 starting price to make the gain comparable with an investment of a different size.</p><div class="formula">Total return = (ending price − starting price + cash distribution) ÷ starting price<small>Multiply the decimal by 100 for a percentage. Here: (52 − 50 + 1) ÷ 50 = 0.06 = 6%.</small></div><p>The price-only return is 4%, while the total return is 6%. A <strong>distribution</strong> is cash paid to the holder, such as a dividend. Include that receipt once. Do not add it again if an already supplied total-return series includes it.</p><p>This one-period formula treats the distribution as cash held to the period end, earning no extra return. Reinvestment within the year would need payment dates and reinvestment prices. Fees and taxes are zero in this example. New money added by an investor is an external cash flow, not investment performance; this simple formula does not handle those flows.</p><h3>Predict before checking</h3><p>A price falls by £4 but the investment pays £2. Does receiving cash necessarily make the total return positive? Explain to yourself, then use the new case below.</p></section>`,reflection:'Explain why price return can differ from total return. State one cash-flow or cost assumption you would record.',criteria:'My method includes a distribution once, divides by the starting value, and states what the example omits.',transfer:'After the first calculation, attempt the second case before inspecting its feedback. Keep the negative sign when the net gain is a loss.'},
compound:{eyebrow:'STEP 03 · 25 MINUTES',title:'Growth has<br>a changing base.',lead:'Successive returns multiply. Their arithmetic average need not describe the growth of your money.',objective:'Calculate compound growth, a cumulative return and an annualised growth rate.',body:`<section class="panel"><h2>Worked example: interest can earn interest.</h2><p>Begin with £1,000. Earn 10% over each of two years, keeping all gains invested. The first year earns £100, giving £1,100. The second earns 10% of £1,100, which is £110. The ending value is £1,210.</p><div class="formula">Future value = starting amount × (1 + annual rate)<sup>years</sup><small>Use rates as decimals: £1,000 × 1.10² = £1,210. The rate here is an annual effective rate: the full growth over one year.</small></div><p>With <strong>simple interest</strong>, interest is calculated only on the original principal: £1,000 × (1 + 0.10 × 2) = £1,200. That comparison assumes interest is not reinvested and earns no interest itself.</p><h3>Changing returns</h3><p>When yearly returns differ, multiply their growth factors. A +50% year followed by a −50% year leaves £100 × 1.50 × 0.50 = £75. The arithmetic mean return is 0%, but the cumulative return is −25%.</p><div class="formula">Cumulative return = (1 + r₁) × (1 + r₂) × … − 1<small>All r values are decimals. Multiply the final result by 100 for a percentage.</small></div><h3>Describe a multi-year result on an annual basis.</h3><p>The <strong>compound annual growth rate (CAGR)</strong> is the constant annual rate that would connect a starting value to an ending value. For exactly three years, £1,000 growing to £1,728 has CAGR (1,728 ÷ 1,000)<sup>1/3</sup> − 1 = 20%.</p><div class="formula">CAGR = (ending value ÷ starting value)<sup>1 / years</sup> − 1<small>Assume no external deposits or withdrawals. A calculator's power key may be labelled ^ or xʸ. A square root is the power 1/2.</small></div><p>Annualising describes a result; it does not forecast the next year or describe volatility. In this chapter periods are whole, equally spaced years. Monthly rates and quoted nominal annual rates need a different frequency conversion; do not silently treat them as annual effective rates.</p><h3>Predict</h3><p>Would a +20% year exactly cancel a −20% year? Think about the amount on which the second percentage acts before calculating below.</p></section>`,reflection:'Describe the error in adding successive yearly returns. Explain one thing CAGR does not tell you.',criteria:'I multiplied growth factors, distinguished CAGR from an arithmetic mean, and did not call an annualised result a forecast.',transfer:'Use the first task to practise the multiplier, the second to diagnose an error, and the third to infer an equal annual rate.'},
discount:{eyebrow:'STEP 04 · 30 MINUTES',title:'Put cash flows<br>on the same date.',lead:'Compounding moves money forward. Discounting expresses future money on a present-date basis.',objective:'Calculate a lump-sum present value and sum separately discounted bond cash flows.',body:`<section class="panel"><h2>Worked example: reverse the growth calculation.</h2><p>A hypothetical payment of £1,210 is due exactly two years from today. At a chosen 10% annual effective discount rate, the equivalent amount today is £1,210 ÷ 1.10² = £1,000. We have reversed the earlier compounding calculation.</p><div class="formula">Present value = future payment ÷ (1 + discount rate)<sup>years until payment</sup><small>The result is in time-0 pounds. The discount rate is supplied for this teaching comparison, not a recommended investment rate.</small></div><p>A future pound is not automatically worth less in every possible rate environment. Here we use nonnegative rates. The choice of discount rate reflects the comparison and the cash flow's characteristics; a formula alone does not make an uncertain payment guaranteed.</p><h3>A bond is a schedule, not one undated number.</h3><p>Consider a hypothetical two-year bond: it pays £50 at the end of year 1, then £1,050 at the end of year 2. The final payment includes the last £50 coupon and £1,000 principal repayment. With a supplied 5% rate:</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Worked bond: cash-flow timing and present values</caption><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Payment</th><th>Calculation</th><th>Time-0 value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>End of year 1</td><td>£50</td><td>50 ÷ 1.05</td><td>£47.62</td></tr><tr><td>End of year 2</td><td>£1,050</td><td>1,050 ÷ 1.05²</td><td>£952.38</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>£1,100 across two dates</td><td>Add discounted payments</td><td>£1,000.00</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Keep full precision while calculating and round the final sum to pennies. Adding £50 and £1,050 first and discounting the whole £1,100 for two years gives the first coupon the wrong date. Adding another £1,000 principal would count it twice.</p><p>Assumptions: annual payments, one constant annual effective discount rate, promised payments made in full, no fees or taxes. Actual bond pricing may require default and liquidity analysis, accrued interest, other payment frequencies and a rate for each maturity.</p><h3>Predict</h3><p>If the discount rate rises while the promised payments stay fixed, should their present value rise or fall? The denominator gets larger, so present value falls for these positive future cash flows. A useful comparison is £1,000 now versus £1,100 in two years: at 5%, the latter is worth about £997.73 today, slightly less than £1,000.</p></section>`,reflection:'Explain why the two bond payments need different exponents, and identify where principal is included.',criteria:'I used the payment dates, discounted each cash flow separately, counted principal once and stated that the rate was an assumption.',transfer:'The new bond has a different coupon from the worked example. Write both discounted terms before entering their sum.'},
inflation:{eyebrow:'STEP 05 · 25 MINUTES',title:'More pounds.<br>More buying power?',lead:'Nominal value counts money. Real value adjusts for changes in the price level.',objective:'Calculate an exact real return and express a future balance in starting-year purchasing power.',body:`<section class="panel"><h2>Worked example: a growing balance can buy less.</h2><p>You start with £1,000 and earn a 5% nominal return. The balance becomes £1,050. If prices rise 8% over the same year, the basket that cost £1,000 now costs £1,080. Your ending balance buys a smaller fraction of the basket.</p><div class="formula">Real return = (1 + nominal return) ÷ (1 + inflation) − 1<small>Use decimals. (1.05 ÷ 1.08 − 1) × 100 = −2.7778%, or −2.78% rounded.</small></div><p>Subtracting inflation, 5% − 8% = −3%, is a useful approximation for small rates, but it is not the exact calculation. It may be misleading when rates are larger or precision matters.</p><div class="formula">Starting-year purchasing power = nominal ending balance ÷ price-level growth factor<small>For one year here: £1,050 ÷ 1.08 = £972.22 in starting-year pounds.</small></div><p>For n years of constant annual inflation π, the price-level factor is (1 + π)<sup>n</sup>. Deflate the nominal ending balance by that factor. This is not the same operation as discounting a future contractual payment using an investment discount rate: state which question and rate you are using.</p><h3>Make the units explicit.</h3><p>A chart showing £1,600 in year 10 may mean nominal pounds, starting-year purchasing power, or a discounted present value. These are different quantities. Always label the date basis and the rate convention. A broad inflation index is also an approximation to an individual's spending experience.</p><p>The lab uses assumed annual effective rates, constant inflation, reinvested growth and no additional deposits. None of its rates is current market data. It has no forecast uncertainty or investment volatility, and it does not model taxes or fees.</p><h3>Predict</h3><p>Keep the nominal growth rate fixed and raise only inflation. What happens to the nominal ending balance? What happens to purchasing power? Write your expectation before opening the lab.</p></section>`,reflection:'Explain why a positive nominal return can have a negative real return. Distinguish an inflation adjustment from discounting at an investment rate.',criteria:'I used the ratio formula for real return, labelled the value in starting-year pounds, and stated that the rates are hypothetical.',transfer:'Use the second task to report a pound amount rather than a percentage. Keep the units distinct.'}
};
